  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Thursday/...\nIssued 155 PM MDT THU AUG 27 2026\nAs one trough moves east, another one slots in along the coast.\nOn Sunday, this puts us into the dry deformation zone, and \nallows temperatures to climb into Monday. Sunday will be one of \nthe cooler days in the long term about 10 degrees below normal, \nwarming up to near normal on Monday. Monday evening, the coastal\ntrough moves inland, and could produce storms in S-Central \nIdaho. The bulk of the activity will be over the four-corners \nstates, so models vary on how far the northwestern fringe moves \ninto Idaho, but it does appear to be a potent shortwave. Storms \nwill be capable of heavy rainfall, small hail, and gusty winds. \nTuesday evening, yet another trough moves in the Pacific NW \ncoast for storms over Central Idaho and Eastern Oregon. The \ntrough comes ahead of the core of the low pressure, set to move \nin later in the week with very low heights, a strong pressure \ngradient, a big cool down and some potentially strong winds. \nUncertainty remains elevated in the extended period, as tropical\nactivity in the Pacific enhances the jet stream. Models do not \nhandle the energy transfer well in these situations so expect \nlots of changes in the far extended period over the next week \nwith all these tropical systems.",
